# REGRESSION FIXTURE — the cheapest differential in this family: TWO SPELLINGS
# OF ONE INTENT that behaved OPPOSITELY, both `gg check`-clean, in one program.
#
#   inc(&v[0])                  -> was WRONG for the six by-value types
#   inc(&v.get(0).unwrap())     -> was CORRECT for all ten
#
# The get-chain spelling was right by a different mechanism: `.get()` returns a
# `Ptr(T)`, so the read already yielded a pointer and the `is_already_ptr`
# fall-through forwarded it. The index spelling read a value COPY. Nothing in the
# language distinguishes the two — same collection, same element, same sigil —
# so the split was pure implementation accident.
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12): the SPELLING axis of one intent, at the `Vector[int]`
# element cell — the value-typed element, which is the side that was broken. Both
# halves are asserted in the same program so a future change that fixes one and
# breaks the other cannot pass.
#
# RED-VERIFIED, and note the two halves need DIFFERENT forms of red (Core #12's
# ⚠ clause):
#   * The `&v[0]` half: RED against the pre-fix compiler, which printed `10`.
#   * The `&v.get(0).unwrap()` half was ALREADY GREEN at HEAD (printed `142`), so
#     no pre-fix compiler can red it. Break-and-watch instead: delete the
#     `is_already_ptr` fall-through guard in `lower_call_arg`'s `MutableBorrow`
#     arm and this half emits `**T`, so the callee reads pointer bits as payload
#     and the assertion fails. That guard is what this half pins — the fix must
#     NOT remove it, because unresolvable shapes still land there.
#
# GUARDED MECHANISM: the index half pins `try_resolve_place`'s `Index` arm and
# its `Projection::Deref` normalisation (postcondition 1); the get-chain half
# pins the surviving `is_already_ptr` fall-through.
#
# Seeds are computed, never literals.
int seed():
    return 10

void inc(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void setv(int &n):
    n = 142

void main():
    Vector[int] v = Vector[int]()
    v.push(seed())

    # Spelling A — subscript. Was silently wrong.
    inc(&v[0])
    print(v[0])

    # Spelling B — get-chain. Was already correct; pins the fall-through guard.
    setv(&v.get(0).unwrap())
    print(v[0])
